## RabbitMQ Guide

Since this project incorporates RabbitMQ as a Pub/Sub service, you must adhere to the following instructions before running the app.  
A `docker-compose.yml` file is included in this project which will be used for creating a RabbitMQ container. Some commands are also added to the `Makefile` file for running a container (The following guide is applicable if GNU/Make tool is installed on your machine).

### Run A RabbitMQ Container

First, make sure Docker is up and running on your machine. Then in the root path of the project run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-up
```

In order to stop the container, you can run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-down
```

### RabbitMQ Web Interface

RabbitMQ comes with a web-based management tool that is accessible at `http://localhost:15672` for management tasks such as publishing new messages or consuming published messages. You can use `guest` as both username and password in the login form.

## Publish A New Message

By running the following command in the terminal, open the RabbitMQ CLI:

```text
$ docker-compose exec rabbitmq /bin/bash
```

Then you need to make sure RabbitMQ management plugin is enabled. You can enable it by running:

```text
rabbitmq-plugins enable rabbitmq_management
```

The generated worker is a single long-running process. Its `main` opens the broker, publishes one sample message on startup, and then consumes every message on the queue until you stop it with Ctrl+C:

```go
broker, err := rabbitmq.New("testing", logger)
if err != nil {
	logger.Fatal(err)
}
defer func() { _ = broker.Close() }()

// Publish a sample message. The domain entity carries no wire-format tags, so
// the composition root maps it to a transport payload before marshalling.
sample, _ := json.Marshal(struct {
	Data string `json:"data"`
}{Data: "Hello World"})
_ = broker.Produce(ctx, sample)

// Consume every message until the process is stopped.
go broker.Consume(ctx, func(value []byte) {
	logger.Info("message received", "payload", string(value))
})
```

We use the name `testing` for the queue. Running `make run` publishes one message to the queue and then consumes it, logging the payload. You can inspect the queue from the RabbitMQ CLI (note it only reports the count, not each payload) or the web interface at `http://localhost:15672/#/queues`:

```text
rabbitmqadmin get queue=testing
```

## Send More Messages

The worker already consumes the queue, so to send more messages, publish from the RabbitMQ CLI:

```text
rabbitmqadmin publish routing_key=testing payload="Hello World"
```

The terminal window running the worker logs the payload:

```text
message received payload=Hello World
```

## Publisher-Only and Subscriber-Only Workers

To specialize a worker, keep only the half you need in `main`: delete the `broker.Consume(...)` goroutine for a pure publisher, or delete the `broker.Produce(...)` call for a pure subscriber. You can also scaffold two separate workers:

```text
$ gopamin new -n publisher -t worker -b rabbitmq -l {{.Logger}}
$ gopamin new -n subscriber -t worker -b rabbitmq -l {{.Logger}}
```

Edit each to keep just its half, then run both with `make run` (start the subscriber first so it doesn't miss the publisher's message). The subscriber logs each payload it receives:

```text
$ make run
message received payload={"data":"Hello World"}
```